{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NgnzRxo84ENEYmDfuKYbzn","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t\n     2\t**2.** ⚪ You delete a Pod that a ReplicaSet owns. Describe what happens next and what caused it.\n     3\t\n     4\t**3.** ⚪ You write a Deployment manifest in which the Pod template's labels do not match the Deployment's selector, and you `kubectl apply` it. What happens?\n     5\t\n     6\tA) The Deployment is created and produces a growing population of Pods it cannot see.\n     7\tB) The Deployment is created but reports zero replicas forever.\n     8\tC) The API rejects the object.\n     9\tD) The selector is silently rewritten to match the template.\n    10\t\n    11\tD) The requested count in `metadata`; the running count in `spec`\n    12\t\n    13\t---\n    14\t\n    15\t**Q3.** 🔵 You submit a Deployment manifest whose `.spec.selector` does not match `.spec.template.metadata.labels`. What happens?\n    16\t\n    17\tA) The Deployment is created and enters a Pod-creation loop it cannot terminate\n    18\tB) The API rejects the object\n    19\tC) Kubernetes copies the template labels into the selector\n    20\t**Q13.** 🔵 Which of these workloads most clearly requires a StatefulSet rather than a Deployment?\n    21\t\n    22\tA) A web front end that caches rendered pages to a local disk\n    23\tB) An image-resizing service that writes temporary files while processing\n    24\tC) A three-member clustered datastore whose members address each other by stable hostname and each own distinct data\n    25\tD) A batch importer that writes its output to object storage\n    26\t\n    27\t---\n    28\t\n    29\t**Q17.** 🟡 A StatefulSet's Pods, like a Deployment's, are found by a label selector. Given that, what does the selector *not* tell you about a StatefulSet's Pods?\n    30\t\n    31\tA) Which namespace they are in\n    32\tB) Which of them is which — the selector identifies the set, not the identity of members within it\n    33\tC) Whether they are running\n    34\tD) How many there should be\n    35\t\n    36\t---\n    37","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0921fb15-519d-409d-959f-bac6f0987a7b","uuid":"45e67c81-927c-4c8e-9e2e-3a8d315dbb3b","timestamp":"2026-08-24T15:39:30.444Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t\n     2\t**2.** ⚪ You delete a Pod that a ReplicaSet owns. Describe what happens next and what caused it.\n     3\t\n     4\t**3.** ⚪ You write a Deployment manifest in which the Pod template's labels do not match the Deployment's selector, and you `kubectl apply` it. What happens?\n     5\t\n     6\tA) The Deployment is created and produces a growing population of Pods it cannot see.\n     7\tB) The Deployment is created but reports zero replicas forever.\n     8\tC) The API rejects the object.\n     9\tD) The selector is silently rewritten to match the template.\n    10\t\n    11\tD) The requested count in `metadata`; the running count in `spec`\n    12\t\n    13\t---\n    14\t\n    15\t**Q3.** 🔵 You submit a Deployment manifest whose `.spec.selector` does not match `.spec.template.metadata.labels`. What happens?\n    16\t\n    17\tA) The Deployment is created and enters a Pod-creation loop it cannot terminate\n    18\tB) The API rejects the object\n    19\tC) Kubernetes copies the template labels into the selector\n    20\t**Q13.** 🔵 Which of these workloads most clearly requires a StatefulSet rather than a Deployment?\n    21\t\n    22\tA) A web front end that caches rendered pages to a local disk\n    23\tB) An image-resizing service that writes temporary files while processing\n    24\tC) A three-member clustered datastore whose members address each other by stable hostname and each own distinct data\n    25\tD) A batch importer that writes its output to object storage\n    26\t\n    27\t---\n    28\t\n    29\t**Q17.** 🟡 A StatefulSet's Pods, like a Deployment's, are found by a label selector. Given that, what does the selector *not* te